WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:04.237
New Troy: The Staggering&nbsp;
Siege of Ostend (1601–1604)&nbsp;

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:10.160
On July 5, 1601, Archduke Albert gave the&nbsp;
order, and the air above Ostend erupted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:16.000
in thunder. Fifty massive field guns roared,&nbsp;
shaking the dunes of Flanders to their core,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.480
while 12,000 Spanish soldiers surged forward,&nbsp;
intent on bringing the city to its knees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.560
Little did they know that this was only&nbsp;
the opening act of a merciless war of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.560
attrition—a siege that would grind on&nbsp;
for more than three relentless years.&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.000
The siege of Ostend was one of the longest and&nbsp;
bloodiest in history. Contemporaries even referred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.840
to it as the “New Troy,” evoking the legendary&nbsp;
siege. All of Europe was captivated by the events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.680
and hundreds of onlookers traveled to the coast&nbsp;
of Flanders to witness the spectacle firsthand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:58.720
Some even ventured into the city to watch the&nbsp;
action up close. And there was indeed much to see:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:06.160
Ostend saw siege engines the world had never&nbsp;
witnessed before, and an Italian strategist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:13.200
demonstrated that bookworms should never be&nbsp;
underestimated. This is how modern historiography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:26.000
tells the story of the staggering siege of Ostend.
Chapter 1: A Thorn in Spain’s Side&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.640
The Dutch enclave around Ostend was a thorn&nbsp;
in Spain’s side. As long as the Dutch Republic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.760
controlled the area, Archduke Albert—who ruled&nbsp;
the Spanish Netherlands alongside his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.520
Isabella—was forced to wage war on his&nbsp;
own soil and forgo one of the region’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:49.680
most important ports. On top of that, he feared&nbsp;
that Maurice of Orange, the captain-general of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:56.800
the Republic’s armed forces, might use Ostend as&nbsp;
a bridgehead for a southern advance. Maurice had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.640
already led a campaign in that direction&nbsp;
in 1600, dealing Albert a bruising defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.640
at the Battle of Nieuwpoort, one of the few&nbsp;
major field battles of the Eighty Years’ War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.160
Determined to avenge this loss and eliminate&nbsp;
the bridgehead threat once and for all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:21.120
the Spanish regent set out in 1601 to capture the&nbsp;
Dutch Republic’s last stronghold in the south.&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.000
Over the course of the previous century, Ostend&nbsp;
had transformed from a humble fishing village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.760
of just under 3,000 inhabitants into one of&nbsp;
the region’s most formidable military ports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:38.080
By 1601, it was not only a thriving&nbsp;
town but also a powerful fortress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:46.400
garrisoned by 4,500 men and shielded by extensive&nbsp;
outworks. These outlying fortifications enclosed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:53.680
two rings of walls with massive bastions, each&nbsp;
guarded by a marshy moat brimming with seawater.&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:00.640
Ostend’s defenses were formidable, but what made&nbsp;
the city nearly impregnable was its location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:07.040
Water surrounded it on all sides. To the north,&nbsp;
the walls met the sea itself. To the east and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:13.680
west, two wide creeks hemmed in the bastions—the&nbsp;
old harbor on the west, the new harbor on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.080
east. To the south, the outworks stretched&nbsp;
deep into a tangled wetland, crisscrossed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.960
dozens of small channels and streams. Only the&nbsp;
sandy dunes remained dry, offering a place for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:33.040
heavy artillery—but even there, every attacker&nbsp;
confronted the daunting prospect of crossing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:39.440
one of the two harbors at its widest point.
Perhaps the greatest advantage for the defenders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.320
was that the city maintained a lifeline to&nbsp;
Zeeland and Holland through the new harbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:50.640
called Geule (chölä), which roughly translates&nbsp;
to “gully.” Throughout the siege, ships carrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.560
reinforcements, food, ammunition, and other&nbsp;
supplies reached Ostend through this route.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:03.760
Cutting it off was almost impossible. Shifting&nbsp;
tides and treacherous sands made an approach from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:13.200
the east perilous, the Spanish ravelin loomed over&nbsp;
the harbor, and the Dutch fleet ruled the sea. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.280
times, the route was even considered safe enough&nbsp;
to allow civilians—including women—to enter the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:36.160
city and witness the siege up close.
Chapter 2: A War of Attrition&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.640
The Spanish appeared outside Ostend on July&nbsp;
5. Albert chose to attack from the southwest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.400
the more accessible side of the city, even&nbsp;
though it was heavily fortified. The approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.200
was feasible, but clearing the defenders from&nbsp;
their forward positions in the marshes would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:01.040
take time. At this point, Albert had no idea&nbsp;
just how long the operation would drag on.&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:06.000
As the first approach trenches inched toward&nbsp;
the town, more and more soldiers poured into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:12.640
the Spanish camp. Soon, the garrison faced a&nbsp;
vastly superior force of around 20,000 men.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.960
Despite the odds, Charles van der&nbsp;
Noot, the city’s military governor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:23.760
ordered a sortie with just under 1,000 men on&nbsp;
July 12 to probe the Spanish positions. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:30.000
attack revealed just how well Albert’s troops&nbsp;
had prepared: the garrison alone could not hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.360
to dislodge them from their trenches. Van der&nbsp;
Noot immediately wrote to the States General in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:40.960
The Hague—the supreme governing body of the&nbsp;
Dutch Republic—requesting additional troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.720
and urging them to reinforce his garrison&nbsp;
to 8,000 men. Determined to hold Ostend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:52.000
the Dutch acted swiftly, and the first&nbsp;
reinforcements arrived only three days later.&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.280
Accompanying the first troops to arrive was the&nbsp;
man appointed by the States General to lead the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:04.720
defense: Sir Francis Vere. The seasoned English&nbsp;
general had been chosen for two reasons. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:10.320
his presence in the city ensured that Queen&nbsp;
Elizabeth I of England would not object to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:16.880
deployment of English troops. Second, Vere was&nbsp;
fiercely ambitious: by successfully defending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:23.120
Ostend, he hoped to surpass Maurice of Orange,&nbsp;
the Dutch Republic’s captain general. Maurice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:29.360
was far from pleased with Vere’s appointment,&nbsp;
particularly when his rival requested additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:35.040
troops for the defense—troops that had to be&nbsp;
pulled from the field army with which Maurice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.640
was then besieging the town of Rheinberg.
Nevertheless, the captain-general sent the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:47.280
requested 2,000 men to Ostend in mid-July.&nbsp;
But when Vere requested another 2,000 men two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.920
weeks later, Maurice refused and told the States&nbsp;
General that a choice had to be made. If he sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:07:00.560
more troops to Ostend, his field army would no&nbsp;
longer be operational. That left only two options:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:07.120
either Vere received enough men to defend the&nbsp;
city without outside help, or the field army was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:13.360
sufficiently reinforced to attack a key Spanish&nbsp;
fortress, forcing Albert to abandon the siege.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.120
The second option would have required&nbsp;
recruiting additional troops—a costly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:25.040
measure the States General did not want—so&nbsp;
they rejected Maurice’s recommendation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:31.120
ordered him to send the additional 2,000&nbsp;
men to Ostend and return to The Hague.&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:37.120
With these reinforcements, the garrison reached&nbsp;
its intended strength of 8,000 men. Vere was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:42.400
tasked with deploying them to actively&nbsp;
defend Ostend from its extensive outworks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:48.160
The city was among the first in the Netherlands&nbsp;
to boast more than simple ravelins and bastions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:54.160
and the defenders now exploited this advantage to&nbsp;
drag out the siege and bleed Spanish resources.&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:00.640
Albert responded with cannonballs. The Spanish&nbsp;
guns mercilessly pounded the outer defenses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:07.120
On August 14, during a particularly fierce&nbsp;
bombardment, Vere was struck in the head. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.240
men rushed him to the city center for treatment,&nbsp;
but the injury was so severe that he had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:19.040
taken to Middelburg to recover. The loss of their&nbsp;
leader threw the defense into crisis. With Vere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:25.120
out of action, the States General had no choice&nbsp;
but to adopt the strategy Maurice had proposed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:31.760
They sent Major Peter Sedlnitzky to Ostend to&nbsp;
handle matters arising from Vere’s absence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:38.960
while Maurice was authorized to raise another&nbsp;
6,000 men and lay siege to ’s-Hertogenbosch.&nbsp;

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:43.680
At the same time, the States General were&nbsp;
searching for a sustainable way to keep Ostend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:51.120
supplied with troops. It was estimated that the&nbsp;
“garrison would lose 1,000 men a month to death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:58.080
wounds and disease.” Sending fresh recruits&nbsp;
every month was logistically impossible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.760
so they decided to rotate the troops, allowing&nbsp;
soldiers to recover while maintaining an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:08.400
active garrison of 4,000 men. This strategy&nbsp;
followed Maurice’s recommendation. Unlike Vere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:14.160
he believed the garrison should be no larger than&nbsp;
necessary, as the autumn storms would soon make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:21.520
Ostend difficult or impossible to reach for&nbsp;
weeks on end. A larger garrison, he argued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:27.920
would burn through supplies far too quickly.
In addition, Ostend was never designed to house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:34.720
such a large number of soldiers. Even with a&nbsp;
garrison of 4,000 men, 30 to 50 men sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:41.760
had to share a single room. In these overcrowded&nbsp;
conditions, disease spread rapidly, and nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:47.440
every shot from the Spanish artillery found its&nbsp;
mark. To address at least part of the problem, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:53.360
defenders built additional shelters in the outer&nbsp;
works. To shield them from Spanish bombardment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.320
they covered the shelters with a thick layer&nbsp;
of earth, giving them a striking resemblance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:05.680
to the earth bunkers of the First World War.
Albert’s men were struggling as well. Bullets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:11.200
and disease were taking their toll, morale&nbsp;
was falling, and the soldiers from Italy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:19.280
Spain—unaccustomed to Flanders’ harsh climate—were&nbsp;
suffering in the wet winter cold. Increasingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:25.520
men decided they could no longer endure these&nbsp;
conditions and deserted. The battle for Ostend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:44.160
had become a true war of attrition.
Chapter 3: A Stratagem of War&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:50.800
On his return to Middelburg, Sedlnitzky met&nbsp;
with Maurice and Vere to report his findings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:56.160
In his assessment, Ostend could hold out for&nbsp;
the time being, but the diversionary attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:02.000
on ’s-Hertogenbosch had to be launched&nbsp;
without delay. After the meeting, Vere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:07.040
now recovered from his injury, hurried back to&nbsp;
Ostend, while Maurice traveled to The Hague to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:11.520
prepare the diversionary assault.
A few weeks later, on the night of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:18.240
November 1–2, the captain-general arrived at&nbsp;
’s-Hertogenbosch with his cousin Wilhelm Ludwig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:25.600
and some 13,000 men. Their army was too small&nbsp;
to fully encircle the city, so they attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:31.120
bring it to its knees with relentless bombardment,&nbsp;
focusing on the residential areas in the hope of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:37.840
turning the population against the Spaniards. When&nbsp;
no uprising materialized and with the first frost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:43.680
of winter making digging trenches and constructing&nbsp;
fortifications increasingly difficult, the cousins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:49.600
were forced to accept that nothing more could&nbsp;
be done. On November 26, they called off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:56.080
siege and sent their troops into winter quarters.
Because the army of the Dutch Republic never posed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.760
a real threat to ’s-Hertogenbosch, the Spaniards&nbsp;
were able to continue the siege of Ostend without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:07.600
interruption. Their trenches soon reached the&nbsp;
outworks on the southwest side, and a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:14.560
battery dominated the western dunes, pounding at&nbsp;
the Sandhill Bastion. According to Henry Hexham,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:20.880
an English military writer and captain, the&nbsp;
cannonade was so violent that “the work might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:27.120
rather have been called iron-hill than sandhill:&nbsp;
for it was stuck so full of bullets, that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:34.000
of them tumbled down into the fausse-braye.”&nbsp;
But Ostend’s guns took their toll as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:42.800
killing many attackers. Soon the ground before the&nbsp;
ramparts was “strewn everywhere with arms, legs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:49.120
and hands . . . surgeons came out of the town,&nbsp;
and brought back bags full of human fat which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:56.080
they had stripped from the bodies.” As Christopher&nbsp;
Duffy notes, this grisly material was a common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.080 --> 00:13:05.040
ingredient in the preparation of ointments.
On December 23, some soldiers brought a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:11.280
Spanish deserter to Vere. To Vere’s horror,&nbsp;
the man revealed that Albert was preparing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:17.440
major assault with 6,000 to 7,000 men. The&nbsp;
garrison could not hope to withstand such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:23.280
an onslaught—winter storms had blocked supply&nbsp;
ships from reaching the new harbor for weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:30.080
leaving Vere’s men hungry and exhausted. The&nbsp;
outer works would surely fall under such pressure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:35.760
Yet he could not abandon them without a fight,&nbsp;
because that would mean surrendering Ostend’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:43.920
first line of defense. At the last moment,&nbsp;
the Englishman conceived a daring plan.&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:48.640
Three days later, Maurice of Orange could&nbsp;
hardly believe his ears. The defenders of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:55.760
Ostend had invited two Spanish negotiators into&nbsp;
the town and were discussing the city’s surrender!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:02.000
Vere’s explanation that it was a desperate ruse&nbsp;
of war did little to reassure the captain-general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:08.240
But the Englishman knew exactly what he was doing.&nbsp;
As he had explained to his officers a few days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:14.000
earlier, he assured Maurice that the negotiations&nbsp;
were intended only “to amuse [i.e., distract] the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:21.840
enemy for a while, until he received help from the&nbsp;
States General.” In fact, Vere managed to prolong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:29.200
the talks masterfully. Shortly after they began,&nbsp;
he sounded the alarm—even though no attack had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:36.720
occurred—and accused the Spanish of breaking the&nbsp;
truce. Only after a 24-hour investigation did he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:43.680
admit his alleged mistake. To smooth things over,&nbsp;
he then invited the negotiators to a Christmas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:52.080
banquet, which lasted most of December 25. Vere’s&nbsp;
ruse was working better than anyone had expected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:58.240
but the defenders breathed a sigh of relief when&nbsp;
a ship carrying reinforcements finally arrived in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:05.680
the new harbor the next night. The Englishman&nbsp;
then immediately broke off the negotiations.&nbsp;

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:12.560
The Archduke was seething with rage.&nbsp;
Vere had violated the customs of war—and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:20.400
even more infuriatingly, had outwitted him.&nbsp;
Ostend would pay dearly for this audacity.&nbsp;

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:26.160
At the turn of the year, a deafening cannonade&nbsp;
rained down on the city. Hundreds of cannonballs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:31.920
smashed the poorly repaired defenses, paving the&nbsp;
way for the Archduke’s long-awaited retaliatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:38.480
strike. On January 7, after a delay of&nbsp;
more than two weeks, he ordered a major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:45.440
assault. At low tide, shortly after dark,&nbsp;
Spanish troops advanced from all sides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:51.840
most moving through the old harbor in front&nbsp;
of the Sandhill Bastion. They had no inkling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:58.320
that Vere had prepared a deadly trap for them.
Under the cover of darkness, the Spaniards crossed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:05.040
the old harbor with almost no resistance. But as&nbsp;
they neared the walls, a sharp command rang out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:10.800
and hundreds of flaming rings arced over&nbsp;
their heads, bathing them in a flickering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:16.560
hellish light. Seconds later, a deadly&nbsp;
volley from the defenders swept the first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:22.080
brightly lit rank back into the trench.&nbsp;
The second rank advanced immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:28.160
but before the men even came close, a spring tide&nbsp;
surged from their right, sweeping many into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:34.640
sea. The defenders had opened the sluices of the&nbsp;
moat, transforming the accumulated water into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:42.160
devastating weapon. Those who survived the torrent&nbsp;
were scattered and defenseless, picked off one by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:50.800
one. The Spanish assault ended in utter disaster:&nbsp;
nearly 2,000 men perished in a single night.&nbsp;

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:57.760
Ironically, this stunning defensive victory would&nbsp;
become Vere’s undoing. The ease with which Ostend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:04.000
had repelled the assault stood in stark contrast&nbsp;
to his relentless calls for reinforcements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:09.760
The States General began to question whether&nbsp;
the Englishman was truly up to the task. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:15.520
the spring of 1602, they summoned him to The&nbsp;
Hague to explain himself. At the hearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:20.880
Vere became so entangled in contradictions that&nbsp;
the decision was made to replace the garrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:28.080
with 5,500 fresh troops—and to replace&nbsp;
Vere with Colonel Frederick van Dorp.&nbsp;

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:32.400
Throughout the spring, the States General&nbsp;
continued searching for a way to force the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:38.720
Spanish to abandon Ostend. At the end of June,&nbsp;
they finally dispatched Maurice to Brabant with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:44.720
the largest army the Republic had ever assembled.&nbsp;
He was to invade Spanish territory from the east&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:51.520
with some 19,000 infantry and 5,000 cavalry,&nbsp;
aiming to surprise and destroy Albert’s field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:58.320
army under Francisco de Mendoza. But logistical&nbsp;
problems caused such severe delays that Maurice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:04.960
lost any hope of surprising the enemy. By the&nbsp;
time he reached Sint-Truiden, the Spanish were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:10.960
already entrenched and had blocked the road to&nbsp;
Brussels. Maurice tried to lure them into battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:17.440
by deploying his troops in full formation, but&nbsp;
Mendoza stayed behind his earth ramparts. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:24.160
hardly honorable, but brutally effective—the&nbsp;
only way past would have been a costly siege.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:29.200
The captain-general was still searching for&nbsp;
a way forward when supply problems forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:35.440
him to retreat. Determined not to return home&nbsp;
empty-handed, he besieged and captured the small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:42.640
fortress of Grave. Shortly afterward, the States&nbsp;
General disbanded his army for financial reasons.&nbsp;

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:48.480
Maurice’s unsuccessful campaign made it painfully&nbsp;
clear that the Dutch Republic lacked the resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.480 --> 00:19:08.856
to relieve Ostend. For the time being, the&nbsp;
States General abandoned this strategy.&nbsp;

00:19:08.856 --> 00:19:11.200
Chapter 4: Two Bookworms
A deadly yet fruitless routine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:17.680
settled over Ostend. For an entire year, the&nbsp;
Spanish refrained from direct assaults, instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:23.040
erecting a massive artillery platform in the&nbsp;
dunes that unleashed a relentless hail of shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:29.520
upon the city. When the summer of 1602 brought&nbsp;
an unwelcome ally to the Spanish bombardment—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:36.480
plague—the garrison’s losses soared. Only the&nbsp;
tireless efforts of the Republic’s fleet, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:43.200
constantly ferried fresh troops and supplies,&nbsp;
kept Ostend alive for yet another harsh winter.&nbsp;

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:49.360
At the beginning of April 1603, the weather&nbsp;
turned in the Spaniards’ favor. A violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:55.600
storm swept across the coast, ravaging the&nbsp;
seaward defenses. Archduke Albert seized the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.600 --> 00:20:02.240
opportunity. His men drew the defenders’ attention&nbsp;
westward with a feigned attack on the old harbor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:09.920
then hurled themselves with full force against the&nbsp;
southern outworks. The garrison fought valiantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:16.000
but they were forced to abandon the outermost&nbsp;
line—the so-called polder—and fall back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:23.440
ravelins and the main rampart behind it. The loss&nbsp;
of the polder proved catastrophic: from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:29.280
the Spaniards could fire directly into the&nbsp;
city center. Once again, the defenders were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:37.120
crammed together, and there was scarcely a single&nbsp;
place within the fortress safe from Spanish shot.&nbsp;

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:43.280
Although Ostend came under immense pressure after&nbsp;
this defeat, Albert was unable to make any further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:50.720
gains for the rest of 1603. The reason became&nbsp;
painfully clear with every ship that reached the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:57.920
city: as long as the Republic enjoyed unrestricted&nbsp;
access by sea, the fortress could not be starved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:04.640
into submission. To win, the Archduke would&nbsp;
have to cut off the new harbor—but so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:12.400
all his attempts had failed. Then, as if sent&nbsp;
by providence, a gift arrived at his camp:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:20.640
the Pope dispatched one of his most studied&nbsp;
engineers, a man named Pompeo Targone.&nbsp;

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:27.200
Targone immediately set to work at his drawing&nbsp;
board. His mission: to push the approach trenches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:34.240
through the eastern dunes and seize the Spanish&nbsp;
ravelin, whose guns dominated the new harbor. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:41.440
achieve this, he devised all manner of remarkable&nbsp;
contraptions. First came a massive gabion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:47.200
which his men struggled in vain to roll toward&nbsp;
the city. Next, he designed a floating artillery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:51.600
platform with heavy guns that successfully&nbsp;
approached the walls but was sunk by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:58.080
defenders within minutes. Finally, he attempted&nbsp;
a floating dike to block the harbor’s entrance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:06.560
which the first storm washed away. One by one,&nbsp;
Targone’s inventions failed spectacularly.&nbsp;

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:12.560
The Spanish soldiers, still smarting from the&nbsp;
deadly failures of Targone’s experiments, were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:19.280
far from pleased when another Italian “bookworm”&nbsp;
appeared in their camp shortly afterward. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:25.520
young man was the scion of a wealthy Genoese&nbsp;
banking family, well-versed in the theory of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:32.640
war but entirely lacking practical experience. The&nbsp;
hardened veterans shuddered at the thought of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:38.720
this greenhorn might attempt. The Archduke,&nbsp;
however, took an immediate liking to him: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:45.600
young officer not only waived his pay until Ostend&nbsp;
fell, but his family even covered the costs of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:54.080
ongoing siege. In October 1603, Albert placed him&nbsp;
in sole command. This bookworm was none other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.080 --> 00:23:00.320
Ambrogio Spinola, who would go on to become&nbsp;
one of the greatest experts in siege warfare.&nbsp;

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:06.880
The Spanish officers were stunned when the young&nbsp;
man revealed himself as both an exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:13.120
engineer and a charismatic leader. Spinola&nbsp;
quickly earned the respect of the soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:18.960
and considerably enhanced morale in the camp.&nbsp;
He proved to be, as Christopher Duffy writes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:26.400
“that rarest of creatures, a man who had equipped&nbsp;
himself to be a complete commander from the study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:33.600
of books.” Spinola had learned in the library&nbsp;
what others learned on the battlefield—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:38.960
paid for with the blood of their men.
Which is a reminder that sometimes books are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:45.280
the safest way to explore history—preferably from&nbsp;
a comfortable chair, with far fewer cannonballs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:51.120
involved. That idea actually inspired the&nbsp;
colouring book we recently put together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:56.160
Some of the illustrations you’ve seen in this&nbsp;
video—like the musketeer, Maurice of Orange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.160 --> 00:24:02.720
and a 17th century pikemen—are included in it.&nbsp;
If you enjoy the artwork in these videos, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:05.886
lets you take it off the screen and interact with&nbsp;
it yourself. Grab a pen and bring the soldiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.886 --> 00:24:09.840
uniforms, and commanders to life. The book also&nbsp;
features scenes from other historical periods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:15.760
including artwork tied to videos we haven’t&nbsp;
released yet—so there are a few small previews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:38.057
hidden inside. And unlike Ostend, it’s a far&nbsp;
less stressful way to spend an afternoon.&nbsp;

00:24:38.057 --> 00:24:40.400
Chapter 5: The New Troy
Spinola considered an assault on the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:45.920
harbor pointless. In his view, Ostend could only&nbsp;
be brought to its knees by conquering the city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:52.400
meter by meter, gradually pushing the garrison&nbsp;
ever further back. Such a formal siege would take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:59.600
time and cost thousands of lives—but Spinola was&nbsp;
a pragmatist. Even by just blockading the town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:06.080
he was losing men every day. If they were to die,&nbsp;
they should at least make their sacrifice count.&nbsp;

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:11.520
The new commander ordered his troops to construct&nbsp;
covered causeways, known as galleries, across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:18.720
old harbor at several points. He divided the men&nbsp;
by nationality—Germans, Spaniards, Italians, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:24.800
Walloons—and turned the perilous work between the&nbsp;
marshy harbor waters and the defenders’ relentless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:32.080
fire into a competition of honor among nations.
As the men painstakingly advanced, Targone’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:38.480
latest contraption was brought into play: a&nbsp;
massive mobile siege ramp that the soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:46.720
nicknamed the “hell wagon.” Over 30 meters long&nbsp;
and mounted on four enormous wheels, it was hauled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:53.520
toward the walls by 40 horses and a system&nbsp;
of pulleys. At first, it seemed that Targone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.520 --> 00:26:00.000
had finally created a functioning invention—but&nbsp;
then a cannonball shattered one of the wheels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:11.280
rendering the entire machine useless. Another of&nbsp;
the engineer’s devices had failed spectacularly.&nbsp;

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:18.960
In the spring of 1604, Mother Nature once again&nbsp;
asserted her power. A series of violent storms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:23.280
disabled parts of the defenses, which the&nbsp;
Spaniards took advantage of by capturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:30.400
the small polder bulwark. Shortly thereafter,&nbsp;
after nearly five months of grueling labor, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:35.600
Walloon and Burgundian galleries finally reached&nbsp;
the outer wall. Hundreds of men surged across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:41.200
covered causeways, scaled the ramparts, and took&nbsp;
possession of the great polder bulwark and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:48.640
western ravelin. With the outermost fortifications&nbsp;
now in his hands, Spinola was free to launch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:54.720
assault on the city’s first ring of walls.
When the defenders saw their first line of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.720 --> 00:27:02.080
fortifications fall, they set to work constructing&nbsp;
new ramparts and a final refuge. They named their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:07.840
last resort “New Troy,” in reference to&nbsp;
the legendary city said to have withstood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:16.480
a decade-long siege. In this case, however, the&nbsp;
name was far from prophetic. With too little earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:22.800
available, the defenders had to improvise:&nbsp;
they built the ramparts from rubble, soil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:30.640
and the lifeless bodies of their fallen comrades.&nbsp;
The result must have been a terrifying sight.&nbsp;

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:36.720
Back in The Hague, concern over Ostend was&nbsp;
growing. Day by day, it became clearer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:43.680
the city’s fall was only a matter of time, and the&nbsp;
question soon arose of how much longer they should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:51.040
continue investing in a city that might already&nbsp;
be lost. Tens of thousands had already perished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:51.040 --> 00:27:57.280
and it was becoming increasingly difficult to find&nbsp;
men willing to serve in Ostend. Strategically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.280 --> 00:28:03.760
the time had come for damage control—and to&nbsp;
find a replacement for the battered port. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:08.480
Republic needed a stronghold in the south, which&nbsp;
is why the States General turned their attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:15.600
to the well-fortified inland port of Sluis.&nbsp;
On May 23, Maurice marched to the city with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:22.560
19,000 men and began the siege. Thus began a&nbsp;
race against time: could the captain-general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:28.800
secure the new port before the old one fell?
The defenders of Ostend fought with fierce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:34.960
determination, but their strength was waning.&nbsp;
When the Spaniards detonated a mine beneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:41.520
the Porcupine Ravelin on May 29, the garrison&nbsp;
could not withstand the subsequent assault. About&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:48.800
a month later, on June 17, the next advance&nbsp;
struck—this time against two sections of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:54.880
main wall. The defenders threw themselves into&nbsp;
the fight with their last ounce of strength,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.880 --> 00:29:01.360
and a desperate melee erupted. Men stabbed and&nbsp;
slashed at one another—some with the butts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:07.440
their muskets, others with clubs ripped from&nbsp;
the defenses, and still others with their bare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:14.400
hands. Against all odds, the exhausted&nbsp;
garrison held the western wall, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:21.680
southwestern bulwark was lost. The Spanish pressed&nbsp;
forward relentlessly—even the reinforcements that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:29.760
arrived on July 22 could not stem their advance.
After these victories, Spinola grew increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:36.240
confident that Ostend would eventually fall.&nbsp;
He resolved to try to relieve Sluis, hoping to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:44.960
deliver a double blow to the republic. On July 25,&nbsp;
he led 8,000 men against the besiegers, only to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.960 --> 00:29:52.000
repelled by Maurice’s formidable circumvallation.&nbsp;
After that, he tried to seize Cadzand to disrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:59.440
the Dutch field army’s connection to Zeeland,&nbsp;
but this too failed. When Sluis finally yielded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:06.160
to Maurice on August 20, the Republic breathed&nbsp;
a collective sigh of relief. The States General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:13.760
tried one last time to relieve Ostend, but it&nbsp;
was painfully clear that all hope was lost.&nbsp;

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:20.640
On August 22, the weather gods sided with the&nbsp;
Spaniards for the third time. An unusually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:26.800
violent storm for the season tore through the&nbsp;
city, washing away large sections of New Troy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:34.480
Just under a month later, the Spanish overran&nbsp;
the Helmont bulwark and the Sandhill Bastion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.480 --> 00:30:40.160
The moment of reckoning had arrived: the&nbsp;
defenders were forced to retreat to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:46.800
final refuge. But the battered earth ramparts&nbsp;
offered scant protection, and the city’s last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:53.760
governor, Daniel de Hertaing, made the fateful&nbsp;
decision to surrender after only a few days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.760 --> 00:31:03.760
Ostend fell on September 20, 1604.
The beaches of Flanders had become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:09.120
stage for one of the longest and bloodiest&nbsp;
sieges in history. When Archduke Albert and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:14.080
his wife Isabella arrived to inspect the&nbsp;
conquered city, they found a shattered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:21.360
ghostly ruin. The battle for the fortress had&nbsp;
cost Spain some 40,000 men—closer to the toll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:28.320
of a full-scale war than a single siege.&nbsp;
Albert had achieved his goal of eliminating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:28.320 --> 00:31:38.240
the Dutch Republic’s last stronghold in the&nbsp;
southern Netherlands—but at a staggering cost.

